# Recall harness

`InitDocs/` is the initial idea, not the final spec. Canon: `.cursor/skills/recall-expert-harness/canon.md`. Development rules and test honesty apply to every plan and every code change.

## Planning trigger

If the user message contains `@recall:` or `@reacall:`, or asks for a project, milestone, phase, or feature plan:

1. Read `.cursor/skills/recall-expert-harness/SKILL.md` and follow `planning-chain.md`.
2. Do not launch expert subagents. Sign the panel in this chat after one read of the shared files. A subagent per expert re-reads the same canon, rules, InitDocs, and worktree in a fresh context and multiplies the token cost by the panel size. That is forbidden for every plan, review, and follow-up.
3. Write each sign-off once, under `## Expert sign-offs` in the plan. The chat reply is the synthesis. Do not paste the YAML in the chat as well.
4. Do not hide dissent, and do not treat agreement as valid without a checked list.
5. Do not start implementation in that turn.
6. A plan with no execution manifest is not executable. Do not approve it. The manifest names every file path, type and method signature, branch including the failure, literal, Arabic and English sentence, and test assertion. A heading with an empty list does not count.
7. A missing loop order, exception type, or assertion value is a missing line. The implementing agent stops and names that line. It does not choose the loop, the exception, or the assertion. "Match the existing tokenizer", "use the right merge order", and "stop if the ids disagree" are not lines.

## Executing an accepted plan

The execution manifest is the whole task. Edit only the paths it names. Write only the members, branches, literals, and tests it writes out. Do not invent a file, type, helper, refactor, or sentence. A missing path, signature, branch, or sentence is a stop: name the missing line and wait.

The text embedder direction is in-process llama.cpp. Do not add LLamaSharp 0.30.0. Do not spawn llama-server. Do not execute keep_desktop_tokenizer or trusted_tokenizer_loader.

A code review compares the diff to that manifest. Fix a hunk that contradicts a named branch. Revert a hunk the manifest does not name. Do not redesign, rename, or restyle. If the review would rewrite most of the files, stop and amend the plan.

## Docs end every plan

A @recall: or @reacall: plan names every docs/ page the change creates or updates. Execution writes those pages in the same turn, before the work is called done. A catalog quote is not that page. The user does not have to ask. A plan that changes product behavior and names no docs page is not executable, unless a sign-off says the change has no docs surface. Docs use the four status words in-code, decided-absent, open, and forbidden. They do not describe unbuilt behavior as if it runs.

## Closing a task, epic, or phase

When the user says to close a task, epic, or phase, that plan finishes it. The manifest includes every remaining partial and not-started child. After execution that parent is `done` and 100 in the one progress table. A leftover child is allowed only when a sign-off names a blocker, an external dependency, or a push-back the user has not accepted. Do not open another plan to finish the same close.

## Sign-off effort

Write the YAML once, in the plan. Confidence is an honest account of the check. Do not round it up, and do not use a low number in place of doing the check.

The first line under `## Expert sign-offs` is `Direct:` and one or more ids from `panel.md`. An id not on that line is advisory. Do not write an advisory list. Do not search for an expert who is not in that table.

Check each row once. When that check finds a gap the loaded files can close, close the gap in the plan, then score the closed check once. That second score is final and stays honest. A third pass is a loop: do not re-read the harness, do not re-list the experts, and do not think the check through again.

The user is the approval gate. A low score does not reject the plan and does not stop the turn. `mitigation` names the gap. `recommendation` names the action. The synthesis names an alternative. Hand the plan to the user with those three. A rejection keeps the honest scores. The next turn closes the named gaps and scores that check. Do not raise a score so the plan looks ready. Record a `push-back` or `block` once, direct or advisory.

## Git commits

Never add a `Co-authored-by` line to a commit message. That includes `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>` and any other agent or tool trailer. Never use `git commit --trailer`. Use `git commit -m "summary"` only. `.githooks` strips any injected `Co-authored-by: Cursor` line via hidden PowerShell, not Git Bash.

## Otherwise

Read the matching skill before editing that area. Also read `development-rules.md` and `test-honesty.md` before product code.

